{"kind":"assert","text":"Migration readiness for the whole-repository artifacts, measured end to end against the live log today. Recording it because both halves of the AGENTS.md gate are held by a small, nameable set, and because the cost of waiting is now visible as a number.\n\nFirst half, the quiet wave. It reads awaiting: 14 after I retired my own moot request. I resolved every one against git rather than reading the text. Ten are codex's review requests naming heads that are already ancestors of main, which is the request this rests on. Take those out and the gate reads four: 749c854b, my two waiting on codex at bba27f4f and 69eca373, and a11aba87, which carries no head. So the number is not measuring outstanding work; it is measuring dead records, and it cannot fall on its own, because a review of a merged head can never be answered.\n\nSecond half, approved heads out of main. Exactly one: 00b7070d, the leased-activity focus work. Its approval is mine, independent, ratified, not stale and not retired, and the artifact it names is live — so by the letter of the gate it is a genuine blocker rather than a stale approval to be discounted. But it cannot be merged as it stands. It is 196 commits behind main and git reports conflicts, so carrying it produces a new head, and a new head invalidates the approval and returns it to step 3. The artifact is codex's and the commit is hugh's, so this is not mine to carry. It needs a carry and a fresh review, or a withdrawal; either settles the gate, and doing nothing does not.\n\nThird, the size of the job. AGENTS.md records a measurement of live: 37 and anchored: 17. Today it is live: 55 and anchored: 12. The anchored count falling by five is real progress — those are documentation pages that have been restated on live behaviour artifacts. The live count rising by eighteen is the cost of delay: every merge that records at `.` adds one, and each one added is another artifact that can never flare when the behaviour under it moves. The two numbers move independently, which is worth knowing before anyone reads the pair as a single burn-down.\n\nWhat I am not claiming. This does not say the migration should run now, and I could not run it if it should: more than one actor recorded those 55 artifacts, and the fold admits a supersession only from the target's own author or an actor holding ratifier, which among the current participants means hugh. What it says is that the precondition is close, that it is held by ten retirements and one head, and that neither of those is a large piece of work.\n\nOne caveat on the measurement itself, since AGENTS.md asks for it. The same run reports unresolved: 62 of 101 named references. Nearly all of that is ordinary succession — a request naming an artifact that a later merge retired — rather than a malformed reference. I looked rather than assuming, because a placeholder that never expanded would hide in exactly this number; I filed two such records myself earlier today and retired them once I saw them."}